## LEVEL I STATUS

STILL OPEN.

Classification: proved candidate awaiting independent audit, noncanonical.
The analytic results below are new candidates; the exact-lattice vertex-count
exclusion discussed in Section 7 is prior work and is not claimed as new.

## 1. Starting endpoint and implication chain

The original endpoint is every sufficiently large global minimizer containing a
unit triangle. C-II still has its macroscopic-container and high-surplus full-core
branches. Its full-core branch has a disk limit for the actual force hull; its
macroscopic branch does not. The aggregate outer-walk endpoint remains open.

The chain pursued here is

```text
positive contact directions in two narrow bands
 -> equilibrium at an exposed actual loaded vertex
 -> four forbidden intervals of actual stressed diameter directions
 -> a fixed quadratic area deficit of the actual force hull
 -> four quadratic populations outside that hull
 -> exclusion of this approximate-direction full-core subbranch.
```

The attempted extension from the last population statement to Level I fails:
the diameter-carrying core must also be replaced or released. Section 6 gives a
source-level regression against using leading density and positive stress as that
missing bridge. No independently constructed source or scaffold is substituted
for the global source in the proof.

## 2. Dependencies and notation

The proof uses exactly these canonical shared inputs:

- [local structure, Theorem 4.1 and Lemmas 5.1–5.4](../theory/01-local-structure.md):
  normalized KKT equilibrium; diameter endpoints are extreme; positive-contact
  components have loaded vertices; unloaded vertices are contact barycenters;
- [ambient force geometry, AG](../theory/04-ambient-force-and-cap-transport.md):
  the full source is in a radius `D/2+eta` disk and actual diameter midpoints are
  within `mu` of its center;
- [finite Oler inequality](../theory/03-oler-shell.md), isodiametry, and convex
  perimeter bound; the strict global lattice comparison is Theorem 10.9 in the
  shared local-structure module.

The full-core C-II application uses
[`C-FULLCORE-NEAR-DISK`](../targets/target-c/80-global-isolate-concentration-fullcore-disk-audit.md).
The additional topology application DC.3 uses `C-WOODALL` and the Section-57
strong-diameter-row normalization. The main cone and population theorems do not
use ZR/ZF, the Woodall classification, train topology,
contact minimality, a full-support stress, or a lattice chart.

Let `A` be the entire global minimizing source, `n=|A|`, `D=diam(A)>16sqrt(3)`.
Translate its circumcenter to zero. Set

\[
 R=D/2,\quad a_0=\sqrt3/2,\quad
 \eta=3^{1/4}\sqrt D,\quad
 \mu=\sqrt{D\eta+\eta^2}.
\]

Choose any normalized KKT stress `(alpha,beta)`. Define `C_+`, `H_+`, and
`V=V(C_+)`; let `B=V(H_+)` and `F=conv(B)`. Here `V` means the support of
this selected stress, not all source points. Contact-isolated source points and
points incident only to zero multipliers remain in `A`.

In fact `conv(V)=F`. Every positive-contact component has a loaded vertex. If a
linear functional attained a value larger than its maximum on `B` somewhere in
`V`, choose a component maximum. That vertex is unloaded and is a strictly
positive weighted average of its neighbors. All its neighbors must have the same
maximal value. Propagation reaches a loaded vertex, a contradiction. Thus `V`
is contained in `F`; conversely every loaded vertex has positive contact load.

## 3. Finite angular exclusion theorem

Rotate axes so two reference line directions have unit representatives at angles
`0` and `theta`, where `0<theta<=pi/2`. Suppose every positive-contact vector has
angle within `omega` of one of

\[
 0,\quad\theta,\quad\pi,\quad\pi+\theta.
\]

Set

\[
 \tau=2\sqrt{2\eta/D+\eta^2/D^2}+2\mu/D.
\]

Assume `tau<1`, put `epsilon=arcsin(tau)`, and assume

\[
 \Delta=\pi/2-\theta-2\omega-\epsilon>0.
\]

**Theorem DC.1.** The four-band hypothesis can be weakened to containment of all positive contact
directions in the two antipodal intervals
`[-omega,theta+omega]` and `pi+[-omega,theta+omega]`. Thus this is a theorem
about any single projective arc of width `W=theta+2omega<pi/2`, rather than
only two coherent direction families.

No oriented direction of an edge in `H_+` lies in any of
these four open intervals:

\[
 I_1=(\theta+\omega+\epsilon,\ \pi/2-\omega),\qquad
 I_2=(\theta+\pi/2+\omega,\ \pi-\omega-\epsilon),
 \qquad I_1+\pi,\ I_2+\pi.
\]

Each interval has length `Delta`. This is a theorem about actual stressed diameter
pairs of the original source. No lower bound on a nonzero multiplier is assumed.

### Proof

Suppose `w` is such a unit diameter direction, and choose an actual endpoint `b`
with partner `k=b-Dw`. Since `|k|<=R+eta`,

\[
 w\cdot b=w\cdot k+D\ge R-\eta.
\]

Choose an exposed vertex `p` maximizing `w dot x` on `F`. It is loaded, and

\[
 w\cdot p\ge R-\eta,\qquad |p|\le R+\eta.
\]

Consequently

\[
 |p/D-w/2|^2
 \le (1/2+\eta/D)^2-(1/2-\eta/D)+1/4
 =2\eta/D+\eta^2/D^2.
\]

For every positive diameter partner `q` of `p`, AG bounds its midpoint `m_pq` by
`mu`. Thus

\[
 \left|(p-q)/D-w\right|
 =\left|2p/D-2m_{pq}/D-w\right|\le\tau.
\]

Let `s_p=sum_q beta_pq>0`. The normalized diameter resultant

\[
 z={1\over Ds_p}\sum_q\beta_{pq}(p-q)
\]

satisfies `|z-w|<=tau`. KKT equilibrium puts `z` in the cone generated by the
outward positive-contact vectors `p-r`. Every such `r` is in `F`, so
`w dot (p-r)>=0`.

If the angle `alpha` of `w` lies in `I_1`, this half-plane condition removes the
entire antipodal interval `pi+[-omega,theta+omega]`. The remaining cone lies between angles
`-omega` and `theta+omega`. Its distance from `w` is at least

\[
 \sin(\alpha-\theta-\omega)>\sin\epsilon=\tau.
\]

The difference angle is below `pi/2`, so this is the ordinary distance to the
nearest bounding ray. This contradicts `|z-w|<=tau`.

If `alpha` lies in `I_2`, the remaining cone lies between `pi-omega` and
`pi+theta+omega`; its distance from `w` is at least
`sin(pi-omega-alpha)>tau`, again a contradiction. Reversing both endpoint
orientations proves the two antipodal exclusions. QED.

## 4. Area, perimeter, and actual source population

**Theorem DC.2.** Under DC.1,

\[
 \operatorname{area}F
 \le {\pi D^2\over4}-{D^2\over2}(\Delta-\sin\Delta)
       +\pi D\mu+\pi\mu^2,
\]

\[
 \operatorname{per}F
 \le\pi D-2D(\Delta-2\sin(\Delta/2))+2\pi\mu.
\]

With `Z=|A outside F|`,

\[
 Z>{D^2\over2a_0}(\Delta-\sin\Delta)
       -{\pi D\mu+\pi\mu^2\over a_0}-{\pi D\over2}-1.
\]

### Proof

Let `E` contain both orientations of every positive diameter direction and put
`P=R conv(E)`. By DC.1, `P` lies in the radius-`R` disk with four open circular
caps removed. Each missing arc has angle `Delta`, and its cap has area
`R^2(Delta-sin Delta)/2`. These caps have disjoint interiors. The containing
convex body has area
`pi R^2-2R^2(Delta-sin Delta)` and perimeter
`2pi R-4R(Delta-2sin(Delta/2))`. AG gives `d_H(F,P)<=mu`.
The parallel-body area formula, with `per(P)<=pi D`, and perimeter monotonicity
prove the first two inequalities.

Apply Oler to the entire population `A intersect F` in its actual container `F`:

\[
 n-Z\le\operatorname{area}(F)/a_0+\operatorname{per}(F)/2+1
     \le\operatorname{area}(F)/a_0+\pi D/2+1.
\]

The strict global lattice comparison gives `n>pi D^2/(4a_0)`. Substitution proves
the population inequality. QED.

### Asymptotic consequence

Suppose `D` tends to infinity, `theta` tends to `theta_*`, and `omega` tends to
`omega_*>=0`, with `theta_*+2omega_*<pi/2`. Write
`g=pi/2-theta_*-2omega_*`. Then

\[
 \limsup {\operatorname{area}F\over D^2}
 \le {\pi\over4}-{g-\sin g\over2},\qquad
 \liminf {Z\over D^2}\ge{g-\sin g\over2a_0}>0.
\]

For `theta_*=pi/3` and `omega_*=0` these become

\[
 \limsup {\operatorname{area}F\over D^2}\le {\pi\over6}+{1\over4},\qquad
 \liminf {Z\over D^2}\ge {\pi-3\over6\sqrt3}.
\]

Since `n/D^2 -> pi/(2sqrt3)`, the exterior fraction is at least
`1/3-1/pi-o(1)`. Therefore a C-II **full-core** survivor cannot have all its
positive-contact directions in two vanishing bands with angle tending to 60
degrees. More generally, a full-core survivor cannot have all positive contact line
directions in any fixed projective arc of width less than 90 degrees. The limit
of its projective contact-direction set must have no gap larger than 90 degrees.
This includes direction drift that does not admit any single affine lattice
chart. It does not eliminate a carrier with these directions when the
rest of the actual positive core has other directions.

### Theorem DC.3 — an actual linear bipartition of the diameter support

Under DC.1 assume in addition

\[
 \sin(\Delta/2)>2\mu/D.
\]

Then a line through the full source circumcenter separates the two endpoints of
**every** edge in `H_+`. In particular `H_+` is bipartite.

To prove this, let `alpha_*` be the center of `I_1`, and let `t` be its
perpendicular unit direction. Every oriented positive diameter direction `w`
avoids both `I_1` and `I_1+pi`; hence

\[
 |t\cdot w|\ge\sin(\Delta/2).
\]

For an actual pair write `b=m+Rw` and `k=m-Rw`. Since `|m|<=mu`,
`R|t dot w|>mu` makes `t dot b` and `t dot k` strictly opposite in sign.
No loaded vertex lies on the separating line. Thus the sign of `t dot x` gives
one simultaneous bipartition of all positive diameter edges. This uses the
actual pair midpoints; central symmetry of the finite source is not assumed.

For the canonical relative-interior C-II source, weak diameter rows have been
removed, so this is its entire active `H`. The canonical
[`C-WOODALL`](target_c_woodall_diameter_topology_2026-09-10.md) classification
then makes `H` a caterpillar forest. In particular `d_H>0`, and the odd-unicyclic
`d_H=0` branch is impossible. This application uses the exact scopes of
[Section 57](../targets/target-c/57-zr-zf-rigidity-audit.md) and `C-WOODALL`,
not a replacement diameter graph.

Consequently, along a sequence with positive contact directions in a projective
arc of limiting width strictly below 90 degrees, **both** the full-core branch
and the canonical odd-unicyclic branch are excluded. Any survivor is in the
macroscopic-container, caterpillar-forest branch. There is no improvement of a
carrier coefficient in this implication.

## 5. Four macroscopic populations separated from the stressed core

Take a fixed `0<delta<g`. In each forbidden interval choose its centered
subinterval of angular width `delta`; let `t_j` point in its center direction.
Define four geometric source caps

\[
 A_j=\{x\in A:t_j\cdot x>R\cos(\delta/2)\}.
\]

For sufficiently large `D`, each cap is outside `F`, and its points have distance
at least `c_delta D` from `V` for some positive constant `c_delta`. Indeed DC.1
implies

\[
 \max_{x\in F}t_j\cdot x\le R\cos(\Delta/2)+\mu,
\]

so its projection gap from `A_j` is at least
`R[cos(delta/2)-cos(Delta/2)]-mu`, which is a positive multiple of `D`.
The four caps are disjoint for all sufficiently large `D`.

Each cap has a separate population lower bound

\[
 \liminf {|A_j|\over D^2}\ge{\delta-\sin\delta\over8a_0}>0.
\]

Here is the population proof, without a local-density assumption. The rest of the
source is contained in the convex body

\[
 L_j=\operatorname{conv}(A)\cap\{x:t_j\cdot x\le R\cos(\delta/2)\}.
\]

Its diameter is at most `D`, and its area is at most that of the radius-`R+eta`
disk cut by the same half-plane. The removed disk cap has area
`D^2(delta-sin delta)/8+o(D^2)`. Thus Oler and the strict global source lower
count give the claimed inequality. Every point of `A_j` has zero contact and
diameter multiplier in this selected stress, because it is outside `F=conv(V)`.
This does not say its actual contact degree is zero in an arbitrary source.
In the canonical C-II full-contact-support normal form these points are actual
contact isolates.

These are four explicit **geometric** caps. They need not equal four of the
canonical caps between consecutive loaded hull vertices. The canonical
inactive-container concentration theorem can still be applied separately.

## 6. Exact regression against the global extrapolation

Choose `u=(1,0)`, `v=(2/5,sqrt(21)/5)`, so their angle is strictly between 60
and 90 degrees. For an integer `m>=4` define

\[
 V_m=\{iu:-m\le i\le m\}\cup\{iv:-m\le i\le m\}.
\]

The source has `4m+1` core points, precisely `4m` unit contacts, and precisely two
diameter pairs `(-mu,mu)` and `(-mv,mv)`, of length `D=2m`. For different rays,
nonzero integer coefficients give squared distances
`i^2+j^2-(4/5)ij>=6/5`; the opposite-ray formula is larger. Contacts within
one line are the consecutive integer pairs. Put contact multiplier `m` on each
contact and diameter multiplier `1/2` on each diameter pair. At chain interiors
opposite forces cancel; at each endpoint the contact force `m` equals its
normalized diameter force `(1/2)(2m)`. At the shared center both pairs cancel.
Thus KKT, including `sum beta=1` and `sum alpha=D^2`, holds exactly.

Set `s_m=1+m^-2`. Add all sites of the triangular lattice of spacing `s_m`
in the radius-`m-2` disk whose distance from **every** core point is strictly
larger than one. Call this added set `I_m` and let `A_m=V_m union I_m`.
All distances in `I_m` are greater than one. Every added point is an actual
contact isolate. The diameter remains `2m`, with the same two active pairs;
all added points have radius at most `m-2`. The stress extends by zero.
There is no unit triangle.

The count is

\[
 |A_m|={\pi m^2\over a_0}+O(m)
       =\left({\pi\over2\sqrt3}+o(1)\right)D^2.
\]

For completeness: triangular Voronoi cells have area `a_0 s_m^2` and radius
`s_m/sqrt3`. Covering and containment of the radius-`m-2` disk give its site
count `pi m^2/a_0+O(m)`. Sites deleted by the distance-to-core test have cells
inside the `(1+s_m/sqrt3)` neighborhood of the two length-`2m` chain segments.
That neighborhood has area `O(m)`, so only `O(m)` sites are deleted. This proves
the displayed count, rather than inferring it from finite output.

### An analytic strict competitor for every `m>=64`

Let `N_s(r)` count sites of the spacing-`s` triangular lattice in a closed disk
of radius `r`, and put `rho=1/sqrt3`. The unit-chain row `b=0` deletes at least
`2m-6` of the candidate sites. Each of the rows `b=1` and `b=-1` deletes at
least `2m-12` sites, as follows.

Keep half-integers `q=a+b/2` with `|s_m q|<=m-3` and `|q|>=5/2`. There are
at least `2m-8` half-integers before removing the four central values, hence at
least `2m-12` remain. They are in the radius-`m-2` disk since their squared
vertical coordinate is `3s_m^2/4<1`. Their horizontal distance from the nearest
integer on the `u` chain is `1/2-|q|/m^2`. Therefore their squared distance from
that chain is at most

\[
 (1/2-5/(2m^2))^2+3(1+m^{-2})^2/4
 =1-m^{-2}+7m^{-4}<1.
\]

All these sites are deleted. They are on three different rows, so

\[
 |A_m|\le N_{s_m}(m-2)-2m+31.
\]

The triangular Voronoi cells give

\[
 N_1(m-1)\ge {\pi\over a_0}(m-1-\rho)^2,\qquad
 N_{s_m}(m-2)\le {\pi\over a_0}(m-2+\rho)^2.
\]

Thus

\[
 N_1(m-1)-|A_m|
 \ge {\pi\over a_0}(1-2\rho)(2m-3)+2m-31.
\]

Using `rho<3/5`, `a_0>5/6`, and `pi<22/7`, the right side is strictly larger
than `(86m-5029)/175`, positive for `m>=64`. Hence at least `|A_m|` unit
triangular-lattice sites lie in the radius-`m-1` disk. Selecting exactly that many
produces a unit-separated competitor of diameter at most `2m-2<2m`. This proves
nonoptimality for an unbounded family, with a uniform additive diameter gain.
It is a regression against the intermediate inputs, not a disproof of Level I.

The scalar signed stress on `V_m` even has the canonical-looking PSD and
three-dimensional-kernel properties. For a scalar field `f` its energy is the
sum, over the two length-`2m` chains, of

\[
 m\sum_{i=-m}^{m-1}(f_{i+1}-f_i)^2
 -\tfrac12(f_m-f_{-m})^2\ge0.
\]

Equality holds precisely when `f` is affine along each chain. Since they share
one center, the kernel has dimension three and equals `span{1,P_x,P_y}`.
Extending by zero to the source adds isolate kernel directions. The mixed bar
framework nevertheless has an angle-changing flex and fails canonical ZF; in
particular `h=2` violates its linear diameter-edge lower bound.

The exact verifier checks every source pair, the complete contact and diameter
sets, all equilibrium coordinates, stress normalization, and a same-cardinality
triangular-lattice competitor of diameter strictly below `2m` for each displayed
finite instance. Coordinates are in `Q(sqrt3,sqrt21)` and all squared distances
are in `Q(sqrt7)`; signs are decided by rational squares.

This regression is **not** a global minimizer. It shows why leading optimal
packing density, ambient disk roundness, exact positive KKT stress, and two
contact directions cannot replace the canonical rank/global-optimality bridge.
It has `h=2`, not canonical `h>=ceil(D)`, and a subquadratic positive core. The
regression uses full source populations; no isolates are discarded.

## 7. Attacks killed and comparison with current swarm work

1. **Every force hull is a disk.** False. AG gives the convex hull of an
   antipodal subset of a circle. DC.1 explicitly allows a smaller hull and proves
   the resulting exterior population. The regression supplies such a smaller hull.
2. **Exact lattice vertices plus a linear loaded-vertex count.** Valid, but
   already proved in
   [the September 11 affine vertex exclusion](target_c_affine_lattice_boundary_vertex_exclusion_2026-09-11.md).
   Its `O(D^(2/3))` lattice count already excludes the canonical exact-chart
   subbranch, including vanishing shear. This run does not reclassify it as new.
3. **Only the reference two directions occur on a large carrier, so DC.1 applies
   globally.** Invalid. DC.1 requires the band condition on every positive contact
   in the selected source stress. Other grains and interfaces can supply contacts
   outside those bands at loaded vertices. The theorem cannot be transferred from
   a carrier without that missing hypothesis.
4. **A quadratic inactive exterior population contradicts global minimality.**
   Not proved. The exact regression shows the local/scalar inputs do not do so;
   only an additional global source argument could eliminate the canonical branch.
5. **Small positive multipliers are negligible.** Not used. The proof divides by
   the actual positive diameter mass at a loaded vertex. Arbitrarily small mass is
   allowed; all its partners have the same uniform geometric bound.

Open PRs #578 and #579 respectively use affine boundary counts and lattice shadows
of the loaded hull. DC.1 uses contact-force directions rather than proximity of
positions to any lattice. PRs #581 and #586 concern source refill and paired
macroscopic exterior caps. This report does not identify the new caps with their
containers or assume their additional source-replacement hypotheses.

## 8. Regression and scope audit

The unit-square and positive-stress plus-complex regressions have orthogonal
contact directions (`g=0`), so no positive angular exclusion is asserted. The
previous false orthogonal-polygon shortcut is not used. Repeated outer-walk
vertices, bridges, non-Eulerian diameter graphs, disconnected positive-contact
components, and contact isolates do not affect DC.1. No motion is integrated and
no PSD or zero multiplier is used to infer finite motion. The theorem has no
assumption on stress magnitudes and no boundary-order term is discarded in its
finite statements. Its asymptotic applications only claim a fixed quadratic gap.
A regular unit-side pentagon is a separate scope check: it is triangle-free,
its five contact line directions are equally spaced by 36 degrees on the
projective circle, and its diameter graph is an odd five-cycle. Thus local
triangle-freeness cannot be used to supply the missing sub-90-degree contact-arc
hypothesis. No claim about its large-cardinality global optimality is made.
The near-equilateral shear is allowed to vanish at any rate; the gap depends on
`pi/2-theta`, not `theta-pi/3`.

## 9. Exact remaining obligation and frontier effect

The canonical frontier is unchanged pending independent audit and promotion.
The candidate excludes a global approximate-direction full-core subbranch and
the corresponding canonical odd-unicyclic branch. It reduces any surviving
macroscopic, caterpillar-forest case to four prescribed angular
caps with quadratic population and linear clearance from the stressed core.
It supplies neither a universal detour payment nor an exact unit triangle.

A concrete remaining lemma for this route is:

> For a canonical contact-minimal, then diameter-pair-minimal triangle-free
> global source whose **entire** positive-contact stress is contained in two
> vanishing direction bands with acute angle tending to 60 degrees, and whose
> diameter graph therefore has the actual line-cut caterpillar-forest structure
> of DC.3, replace or
> deform the old diameter-carrying core while relocating the populations in the
> four caps of Section 5 to construct a unit-separated source of exactly `n`
> points and diameter strictly smaller than `D`.

Every separation across old/new interfaces and every active diameter row must be
verified. Merely refilling an isolate cap while retaining the old loaded core
cannot satisfy this conclusion. The general full-core route still needs a
payment for aggregate outer-walk detour or a source competitor when additional
contact directions repair the cones.

## 10. Work products and promotion recommendation

The dated report, exact regression script, and script documentation are the work
products. The report is unreviewed and should receive an independent audit of the
support-point selection, cone signs, area currency, and four-cap population proof.
No canonical files or theorem status have been changed. If promoted, DC.1–DC.3
and the full-source cap consequence should be registered with the shared KKT and
AG dependencies. The global and C-II prompts must retain the macroscopic branch.
